# Line endings are CONTENT here, because two generated things are digests of it.
#
# docs/storybook/tw-bridge.css records a sha256 of everything under
# docs/source_examples/, and docs/tools/check-tw-bridge.mjs re-computes that
# digest from the WORKING TREE and refuses to deploy when the two disagree.
# With no attributes file, a Windows clone under the common `core.autocrlf=true`
# checks those sources out with CRLF while Linux CI checks them out with LF —
# so the same commit hashes to two different values and the check can only pass
# on the machine the storybook was last generated on.
#
# That is not hypothetical: it failed the Pages deploy 36 times in a row
# between 2026-08-20 and 2026-08-21 while passing locally every time, so the
# site quietly served a two-day-old build behind a green-looking repository.
# The recorded digest was the CRLF one; CI computed the LF one; the error
# message sent you to regenerate the storybook, which on Windows re-recorded
# the CRLF digest and failed again.
#
# `eol=lf` — not `text=auto` alone — is what fixes it. `text=auto` normalises
# what is COMMITTED and leaves the checkout platform-native, which is exactly
# the state that produced the bug: identical blobs, different bytes on disk,
# different digest.
#
# The same reasoning covers the other two derivations. packages/brand copies
# docs/colors_and_type.css and docs/fonts/fonts.css byte-for-byte and asserts
# the identity, and `npm pack` runs that build — so without this, a tarball
# published from Windows and one published from CI are different bytes for the
# same version. And build-storybook.mjs compiles the .vue captures with
# esbuild, which is only reproducible if its inputs are.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries: never touched. `text=auto` already detects these, but the four
# formats in this repository are worth naming rather than trusting a heuristic
# with 116 PNGs and 33 font files.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
